  "comments": [],
  "script": {
    "segments": [
      {
        "narration": "Someone hauled forty-ton stone heads a hundred miles. Southern Mexico, before 900 BC. Seventeen of them, each face unmistakably a real individual person.",
        "visual_query": "mexican gulf coast jungle",
        "visual_fallback": "jungle river"
      },
      {
        "narration": "The stone is basalt, and it only comes from one place: the Sierra de los Tuxtlas in Veracruz. Over 150 kilometres from where the heads ended up sitting.",
        "visual_query": "veracruz volcanic mountain range",
        "visual_fallback": "volcanic hills"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Here's the problem. The Olmec had no wheels that worked and no animals to pull anything. So how do you move six elephants' worth of rock?",
        "visual_query": "olmec stone head mexico",
        "visual_fallback": "carved stone"
      },
      {
        "narration": "Rafts along the coast and rivers where water allowed it. Then causeways, ramps and ropes. Several heads have flattened backs, as if built for dragging.",
        "visual_query": "tropical mexican river boat",
        "visual_fallback": "river water"
      },
      {
        "narration": "And the headdresses are all different. These are portraits of named rulers, carved by people who had actually met them.",
        "visual_query": "mesoamerican stone monument closeup",
        "visual_fallback": "stone face"
      }
    ],
    "segment_count": 5,
